18738, 1345, 22445, 364, 279, 9665, 725, 13340, 13, 8938, 411, 25423, 369, 6673, 334, 6804, 13185, 310, 24102, 494, 279, 2100, 1558, 7249, 11, 279, 1534, 1414, 682, 279, 1788, 7731, 430, 4472, 45543, 11, 1442, 3384, 1010, 10885, 513, 11330, 303, 18985, 13, 271, 623, 6380, 11, 279, 4478, 446, 13402, 383, 1204, 3428, 279, 9665, 1558, 725, 58498, 513, 11330, 11, 279, 2695, 314, 4162, 279, 9665, 1558, 11, 321, 279, 29997, 314, 279, 2100, 1558, 725, 22188, 1440, 13, 357, 1603, 9665, 1558, 421, 369, 1691, 22330, 1056, 279, 2100, 321, 7309, 46683, 440, 424, 628, 7936, 3349, 19019, 11, 5142, 948, 279, 2100, 1558, 369, 3349, 321, 42903, 369, 12204, 48184, 13, 271, 760, 1330, 1379, 4047, 7715, 279, 4478, 446, 369, 8882, 1056, 24389, 513, 421, 279, 9665, 1558, 369, 2166, 7234, 466, 29463, 25284, 11, 13969, 1599, 311, 23799, 11, 321, 421, 279, 9665, 1558, 466, 22188, 29997, 369, 524, 11533], "content": "We need answer user's request: summarize in about 300 words for general technical audience how speculative decoding speeds up LLM inference: draft model, target verifies several tokens in one forward pass, why output distribution can be kept identical to normal decoding, what determines speedup in practice. End with two most common reasons speedup smaller than hoped.\n\nNeed produce final about 300 words. Need no preamble. Need likely exactly around 300 words? \"about 300 words\". Let's draft ~300. Need ensure ends with two most common reasons. Could be two sentences or list? \"End with the two most common reasons...\" likely final sentence(s) should be those reasons. Need maybe no headings? Direct.\n\nLet's craft. Count words.\n\nDraft:\n\"Speculative decoding speeds up large language model inference by using a small, fast draft model to propose several future tokens before the large target model is run. The draft model generates a short candidate sequence, often greedily or with a simple sampling policy, because it is cheap to evaluate. The target model then processes the original prompt plus the proposed tokens in a single forward pass and computes its own probabilities for every position in that sequence. This lets the target model verify multiple candidate tokens at once instead of generating them one by one, which is the usual bottleneck in autoregressive decoding.\n\nThe key trick is a rejection test that preserves the target model\u2019s output distribution. For each proposed token, the method compares the draft probability with the target probability. If the target model is at least as confident as the draft, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s preferences while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. Because this correction is mathematically equivalent to sampling from the target model alone, the final text has the same distribution as normal decoding, even though some tokens are accepted in bulk.\n\nIn practice, the speedup depends on how often the draft model\u2019s guesses are accepted, the cost of running the draft model, and the overhead of the target model\u2019s verification pass. A good draft model that is much cheaper than the target and highly correlated with it can produce large gains, especially when the target model is large and inference is compute-bound.\n\nThe two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are that the draft model is too weak or poorly aligned, causing many rejections, and that the draft model or verification overhead is not cheap", "top2": [[[1596, -0.002097898395732045], [760, -6.905157089233398]], [[1144, -1.9073668227065355e-05], [4087, -11.452320098876953]], [[4087, -0.17166821658611298], [310, -1.8552448749542236]], [[1156, -0.00890188105404377], [303, -4.764942646026611]], [[579, -0.27144551277160645], [25, -1.7136833667755127]], [[1622, -0.012370673939585686], [3296, -4.7407145500183105]], [[25, -0.31537532806396484], [13, -1.3526926040649414]], [[59987, -0.10028091073